@[expose] public section

/-!
# Analytically unramified Noetherian local rings

Proof of `isAnalyticallyUnramified_iff_minimalPrimes_and_isN1`: for a Noetherian local ring
`(R, m)` with completion `R^ = lim_n R ⧸ mⁿ`, analytic unramifiedness of `R` — reducedness of
`R^` — is equivalent to `R` being reduced together with analytic unramifiedness of every minimal
prime of `R`, and it implies that `R` is N-1.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AnalyticallyUnramified.MinimalPrimeCriterion.solution`: the proof of
  `isAnalyticallyUnramified_iff_minimalPrimes_and_isN1`.

## Implementation notes

Reducedness of `R` follows from reducedness of `R^` because `R → R^` is injective, which for a
Noetherian local ring is Krull's intersection theorem, i.e. `IsHausdorff (maximalIdeal R) R`; that
is `isReduced_of_isReduced_adicCompletion`, stated for an arbitrary ideal along which `R` is
Hausdorff, which is the generality the argument has.

The other three parts are organized around the identification `(R ⧸ I)^ = R^ ⧸ I R^`, valid for
any ideal `I` with `R ⧸ I` local, which is exactness of completion (Tag 00MB). Through it, analytic
unramifiedness of a quotient of `R` becomes reducedness of a quotient of the single ring `R^`.

For a minimal prime `q` of a Noetherian ring, `q` is an associated prime (Tag 02CE), so
`q = {x ∈ R | f x = 0}` for some `f ∈ R`, and `q R^ = {x ∈ R^ | f x = 0}`. An annihilator ideal of
a reduced ring is radical — `f x ^ n = 0` gives `(f x) ^ n = 0`, hence `f x = 0` — so `R^ ⧸ q R^`
is reduced, which is part (2). Conversely, a reduced `R` has `⋂ qᵢ = 0` over its (finitely many)
minimal primes, so `⋂ qᵢ R^ = 0`; a nilpotent of `R^` lies in every `qᵢ R^` as soon as every
`R^ ⧸ qᵢ R^` is reduced, hence is `0`. That is part (3).

Where the source invokes exactness of completion (Tag 00MB) for the last two steps, what is
actually used is only that `R → R^` is flat (`AdicCompletion.flat_of_isNoetherian`), and the two
steps are instances of two facts about an arbitrary flat algebra `S` over `R`, proved here in that
generality: the ideal generated by the annihilator of `f ∈ R` is the annihilator of the image of
`f` (`mem_map_iff_mul_eq_zero_of_flat`), and base change commutes with finite intersections of
ideals (`map_inf_of_flat`, `map_biInf_of_flat`). Both come from right exactness of the tensor
product (`rTensor_exact`, `Ideal.subtype_rTensor_range`) together with
`Module.Flat.rTensor_exact`; neither is in Mathlib.

Part (4) is split into the two facts the source's argument really uses. `isN1_of_faithfullyFlat`
is the descent: N-1 passes from a Noetherian ring `S` to a Noetherian subring `R` over which `S`
is faithfully flat, because a nonzerodivisor of `R` stays one in the flat algebra `S`, so
`S ⊗[R] Q(R)` is a localization of `S` at nonzerodivisors (`IsLocalization.tensor`) and hence
embeds into `Q(S)`, carrying `S ⊗[R] R'` into the integral closure of `S` in `Q(S)`.
`isN1_of_forall_isN1_quotient_minimalPrimes` is the other half: a reduced Noetherian ring is N-1
once its quotients by its minimal primes are, via the embedding of `Q(A)` into the product of the
`Q(A ⧸ qᵢ)`. The fraction field of `A ⧸ qᵢ` is taken in the form `Ideal.ResidueField qᵢ`, which is
an `A`-algebra on the nose, so that the product is an `A`-module without a choice of scalar
restriction; that the two agree is `IsLocalization.algEquiv`. Applied to `A = R^`, whose quotients
by primes are N-2 because `R^` is a Noetherian
complete local ring (Tag 032W), the two give part (4).

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 032Y](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032Y)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 032X](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032X)
